{"type":"tool_call","subtype":"completed","call_id":"call-7b6be9db-4647-4398-9961-07452db10ce5-25\nfc_a6e4e556-431b-9c1c-8d2a-abbb9239f784_0","tool_call":{"readToolCall":{"args":{"path":"/work/ws/sessions/20260822-232925-frontend-dev-1--22b12422/prompt.txt","offset":50,"limit":250},"result":{"success":{"content":"### Step 1: Select ONE Task\n\nA task is already claimed for you: **MARATHON-16**. Work on that one and no other.\n\nID: MARATHON-16\nTitle: SPA: auth, token storage, workspace shell\nStatus: in_progress\nPriority: P1\nType: task\nAssignee: frontend-dev-1\nLabels: frontend\n\nDescription:\nServe SPA from GET / on any node. Sign-up form with visible error on invalid input; auto-login on success. Logout/login restores same workspace. Store bearer in localStorage huddle.token (or accepted fallbacks). Three-pane layout shell: coloured sidebar + message pane + on-demand thread panel. Workspace identity in sidebar. Stable selectors: auth-form, auth-submit, auth-toggle, auth-modal, workspace-create-form, empty-state-create-workspace, join-workspace-*, current-user, logout-btn, workspace-header, channel-list, dms-list. Drivable at 1280x900.\n\nDesign:\n# MARATHON-16 — SPA: auth, token storage, workspace shell\n\n## Summary\n\nReplace the stub `GET /` HTML with a zero-build Huddle SPA served identically from every HTTP node. The SPA owns browser session lifecycle (register → auto-login, login, logout), durable bearer storage under `localStorage` key `huddle.token` (with documented read fallbacks), restoration of the last workspace after logout/login, and the three-pane workspace shell (coloured sidebar + main message pane + on-demand thread panel) with the epic’s stable `data-testid` selectors so CUA/Playwright can drive auth and workspace onboarding at 1280×900. Messaging, channel CRUD UI, and settings modals remain later SPA tasks; this task only mounts their shell slots and wires the seams that already exist (Auth + Workspaces APIs).\n\n## Technical Approach\n\n### Decision\n\n| Concern | Choice |\n|--------|--------|\n| Delivery | **Static SPA** under `/app/static/` served by FastAPI; `GET /` returns `static/index.html` on every node |\n| Frontend stack | **Vanilla HTML + CSS + ES modules** — no Node, no bundler, no React |\n| API access | Same-origin relative `/api/...` (works on `:8000`/`:8001`/`:8002` without CORS or hard-coded ports) |\n| Auth header | `Authorization: Bearer <token>` exactly as MARATHON-3 |\n| Token storage | Write **`localStorage[\"huddle.token\"]`**; read primary then accepted fallbacks (below) |\n| Workspace restore | Persist **`localStorage[\"huddle.workspaceSlug\"]`** across logout; re-select after next login |\n| Routing | **Hash routes** (`#/`, `#/w/{slug}`) — no server path rewrite; refresh-safe on any node |\n| Selectors | Stable **`data-testid=\"<name>\"`** attributes matching the task list exactly |\n| Shell layout | Fixed three-pane: sidebar (coloured) \\| message pane \\| thread panel (hidden until opened) |\n\n### Why\n\n- Epic requires browser UI at `GET /` with stable selectors; MARATHON-2 explicitly deferred SPA to this task and left a stub.\n- Zero-build static assets keep `start.sh` / Python-only ops unchanged (no second runtime), matching MARATHON-2’s self-contained cluster.\n- Same-origin `/api` + shared SQLite tokens already work cross-node (MARATHON-3/22); the SPA must not invent per-node cookies or JWT.\n- Hash routing avoids FastAPI catch-all complexity and keeps `GET /api/*` unambiguous.\n- Writing only `huddle.token` while accepting read fallbacks matches the task wording and MARATHON-3’s named key without breaking graders that seed alternate keys.\n\n### Alternatives considered\n\n| Alternative | Rejected because |\n|------------|------------------|\n| React/Vite SPA with build step | Requires Node in `start.sh` / image; epic forbids remote services and prefers self-contained boot |\n| Cookie/session auth | Contradicts bearer + `huddle.token` contract already locked by MARATHON-3 |\n| Path-based SPA routes (`/w/acme`) without catch-all | Refresh on deep links 404 unless every node mounts a rewrite; hash is simpler |\n| Server-rendered auth forms | Breaks SPA continuity for MARATHON-17/18 and CUA driving a single document |\n| Invent join/members REST in this task | Owned by MARATHON-6/7/12; SPA locks client seam only |\n\n### Trade-off accepted\n\nJoin-workspace UI is **mounted and wired** to a join REST seam that MARATHON-7 (open-workspace auto-onboard / join) must implement. Until that endpoint exists, submit shows the server error envelope (or a clear network failure). Create-workspace is the fully working onboarding path today. We accept temporary join failures over inventing a second membership SoT in the SPA task.\n\n### Conventions inherited (must not diverge)\n\nFrom **MARATHON-2**: Product **Huddle**; `GET /` is HTML 200 on every node; `/api/...` JSON; no DinD.\n\nFrom **MARATHON-3**: `UserObj` key set; `{error:{code,message}}`; register/login `{user,token}`; `/api/auth/me`; bearer parsing; username/password validation rules; store token as `huddle.token`.\n\nFrom **MARATHON-4**: Same `UserObj`; profile PATCH not required in this shell (settings UI is MARAT\n... [truncated]\n\nNotes:\nDesign complete for SPA auth/token/shell. Zero-build static SPA at GET /; huddle.token + fallbacks; three-pane shell + stable data-testids. Join form wired to POST /api/workspaces/{slug}/join (server owned by MARATHON-7). Ready for lead review — keep frontend label for implementer after architect approval.\n\n\n### Step 2: Ground Yourself Before Building\n\n#### 2a. Read the Epic\n- Determine the parent epic from the task ID or the parent field of 'loom data show <id> --output json'\n- Run 'loom data show <epic-id>' and read its notes — conventions recorded there are **authoritative**\n\n#### 2b. Read Dependencies and Siblings\n- Check the depends_on field of 'loom data show <id> --output json'. If a dependency is still open, go to Step 6a.\n- Read two or three closed sibling tasks in the epic for the component names, prop shapes, and styling conventions they established. Match them.\n\n#### 2c. Read the Design and Reconcile Against the Code\n- Read the design in full: component inventory, states, responsive behavior, accessibility criteria\n- Open every file the design names and read it as it exists on disk right now\n- Run 'git log --oneline -5 -- <file>' on each to see whether a sibling task already moved it\n- Where the code has moved on, follow the design's **intent** and the code's **current** conventions. Where the design is fundamentally unbuildable, go to Step 6b.\n\n#### 2d. Read the Neighborhood\nOpen the components next to the ones you are changing. Copy their patterns: file layout, prop naming, how they import tokens, how they handle async. Consistency here is worth more than your preferred style.\n\n### Step 3: Build\n\n- Work through the design's component inventory. Reuse before you create; create only what the inventory says does not exist.\n- Keep every change inside this task's scope. Do not refactor neighbors you happened to read.\n- Use the existing style tokens and scale steps. If the design asked for a value with no token, add the token the design named rather than scattering a literal.\n- Wire the real data path. Placeholder content that \"will be hooked up later\" is not an implementation.\n- **No TODO comments for deferred work.** If something in the design cannot be built yet, record it in task notes so it becomes tracked work — a TODO in the code is invisible to the board and never gets done.\n\n### Step 4: Verify It Renders\n\nBuild the project first and fix every compile and type error before going further.\n\nThen exercise the UI, do not read it:\n- Before starting a command that binds the app's fixed ports, run `marathon-freeports` if a port is busy, then wrap the command as `marathon-portlock <cmd>`. Four workers share the ports and the lock serializes them.\n- Start the app and open the pages you changed in a real browser\n- Walk each state the design specifies: **loading**, **empty**, **error**, and the happy path. Force them — delay the request, empty the data, break the endpoint. A state you never saw is a state you did not implement.\n- Check the responsive behavior at each breakpoint the design names\n- Check the accessibility basics: heading order, every control reachable and operable by keyboard, a visible focus style, labels on form controls, alt text on images, and text contrast that meets the project's requirement\n- Watch the browser console: warnings and errors count as failures\n- Kill every server you started before signalling completion. Never leave a server running.\n\nRun the project's existing test and lint commands as the repository defines them, and fix what you broke.\n\n### Step 5: Review Your Own Change\n\nRe-read your diff before you deliver it:\n- Does every acceptance criterion in the design hold? Check them one at a time, including the negative ones.\n- Did anything land outside this task's scope? Remove it.\n- Any leftover debugging output, commented-out code, or unused imports? Remove them.\n\n### Step 6: If You Cannot Finish\n\n**Step 6a — External blocker** (nothing in this repository can move it forward: an unfinished dependency, a missing endpoint, an approval):\n```\nloom data update <id> --status blocked --notes \"BLOCKED: <reason + any blocking task ID>\"\n```\nCommit meaningful partial work first, then run 'loom complete' and EXIT. Blocked tasks wait for a human.\n\n**Step 6b — The design is unviable** (it can move forward, but the spec has to change):\n```\nloom data update <id> --notes \"NEEDS-REVISION: <what is wrong with the design + the concrete direction the next version should take + evidence>\"\nloom data update <id> --status open --add-label needs-revision --add-label architect --assignee \"\"\n```\nAdding both labels routes the task back to the architect. Commit salvageable work, run 'loom complete', and EXIT. The design agent role picks it up from your feedback.\n\nIf you cannot tell which applies, prefer 6b: it is non-terminal and cheaper than waiting on a human.\n\n### Step 7: Deliver Through the Harness Gate\n\n- Re-run the project's build, test, and lint commands. Do NOT deliver with anything failing.\n- Stage only your files and commit: `git add <files> && git commit -m \"<brief description> (<task-id>)\"`\n- Do not stage with `git add -A` or `git add .` — another agent's work may be sitting next to yours\n- Record anything the reviewer needs to know (a deviation from the design, a gap you found) in the task notes\n- Determine your attempt number: count existing comments containing `IMPL-DONE` on this task (from `loom data show <id> --output json`) and add 1\n- Record the completion signal (REQUIRED, exact shape):\n  `loom data comment <id> \"IMPL-DONE attempt=<n> commit=$(git rev-parse HEAD)\"`\n- Move the task to review and release it:\n  `loom data update <id> --status review --assignee \"\"`\n- NEVER close the task yourself — the harness gate integrates and closes\n- Signal completion: `loom complete`\n\n### CRITICAL: STOP\n\nAfter Step 6 or Step 7 you are DONE.\n- Do NOT pick up another task\n- Do NOT keep polishing\n- Simply EXIT\n\nYou completed ONE task. You will be run again for the next one.\n","isEmpty":false,"exceededLimit":false,"totalLines":211,"fileSize":14000,"path":"/work/ws/sessions/20260822-232925-frontend-dev-1--22b12422/prompt.txt","readRange":{"startLine":50,"endLine":211},"relatedCursorRulePaths":[],"relatedCursorRules":[]}}},"hookAdditionalContexts":[],"toolCallId":"call-7b6be9db-4647-4398-9961-07452db10ce5-25\nfc_a6e4e556-431b-9c1c-8d2a-abbb9239f784_0","startedAtMs":"1787441767811","completedAtMs":"1787441768113"},"model_call_id":"8050f64d-a0a5-415c-a800-5a4730031dd8-5-cufq","session_id":"8c9f2801-d9d1-4b4e-ac13-8ec9ab25b62b","timestamp_ms":1787441768133}
